<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-8">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/maps2.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4">
                <h3>Publish or perish</h3>
                <p>From the beginning of a project, the work I do is documented in both a design team notebook and a project board for stakeholders. The team notebook documents nitty-gritty details of our research and business needs. Our stakeholder documents are for a wider audience, provide visibility into where we are in a project and how we've arrived at our decisions.</p>
                <p>I also publish a design process overview that helps set expectations and align on workflow, language and the purpose of each part of our work.</p> 
      
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>

<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4">
                <h3>Make a map</h3>
                <p>Every digital product is a living, breathing being that gets messy as it evolves.</p>
                <p>I start a new project making maps that help identify patterns and spot inconsistencies. Map-building is also a way to get a feel for the main flows of a product, much like walking a job site before any build begins.</p><p>This tends to be a deceptively quick process, often involving a single day's worth of screenshotting and arranging, but has been super helpful as a part of the process.</p> 
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-8">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/maps.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>


<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/stories.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-5">
                <h3>Find the stories (and solutions) that haven't been told.</h3>
                <p>I look for the people and real-world spaces where a product is used, and I always make it a point to spend time talking with not only  SMEs, but sales and support staff, engineering, business analysts, and anyone else who works in and around a product.</p>
                <p>I also make it a point to do site visits whenever possible, and frequently get valuable insights into the real world situation in which our users interact with us. In spaces like medical software, these realities can be extremely visceral, and carrying them back to the team gives much-needed context.</p>
                <p>The phrase I hear the most often during this time is 'No one's ever asked us about this before' and it always lets me know I'm on the right track.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-3">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/whoelse2.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>



<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4">
                <h3>If you’ve been paying attention, you’ve already got a million ideas</h3>
                <p>The best part of map-making is that you tend to find ample gold during your process, which is why solutioning always feels more like winnowing down a fix to its essential elements and what it would take to apply it in our problem space.</p>
                <p>I like to work quick and medium-dirty at this stage, and love the lowest-fidelity method that can communicate an idea quickly, whether it be post-its, procreate wireframes, or the trusty whiteboard.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-8">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/millionideas.png" />
            </div>


        </div>   
    </div>
</section>



<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/thingstotest1.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4">
                <h3>Things to test &amp; the sneakiest possible way to test them</h3>
                <p>Code prototyping has been my holy grail for years, and it's the part of modern AI tools for which I'm the most thankful. There's a place for ever tool, and paper, click-through or other prototypes have great utility, but there's something extra, extra special about being able to test in the best apparoximation of the real medium, on a real device.</p><p>Tools like Claude Code are answering a lot of my dreams for performant ways to produce apples-to-apples test candidates to validate solutions.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/thingstotest2.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>



<section class="projectsection dark">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/dialitin1.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-4">
                <h3>Dial it in</h3>
                <p>Getting a frontend that looks like someone really, really paid attention means a great deal to me, Rather than hoping every developer is an obsessive measurer of pixels, this really comes from a well-executed design QA process and component workflow.</p>
                <p>In recent years, I've had the luxury of being able to build out design systems where these details can be dialed in, as well as resources to fine tune code as it makes it journey from test to production.</p>
            </div>
  
            <div class="images col-md-4">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/dialitin2.png" />
            </div>

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>




<section class="projectsection">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
  
            <div class="images col-md-6">
                <img class="image" src="/images/process/operationalize.png" />
            </div>

            <div class="imagemeta col-md-6">
                <h3>Operationalize, democratize</h3>
     

            <p>As a product moves from idea to iteration, design docs, codebases and projects can grow into resources for both internal and external teams. Self-documented Figma files can help new hires onbaord quickly while normalizing expectations around workflow, and spec docs and pattern libraries can become valuable references to let whoever's bulding what comes next (whether human or LLM accessing via MCP) build successfully on existing work.</p>
            </div>
            

        </div>   
    </div>
</section>